  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "Why can't you tickle yourself?",
      "a": "Your brain predicts the feeling of your own movements and turns down touches that match the prediction. A tickle you make yourself is expected, so it feels much weaker than one from someone else.",
      "asks": [
        "Why is self-tickling impossible?",
        "Why doesn't tickling myself work?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What does the cerebellum do?",
      "a": "The cerebellum, at the back of the brain, helps coordinate smooth movement. It also predicts what your movements will feel like, so the brain can tell self-made sensations from outside ones.",
      "asks": [
        "What is the cerebellum?",
        "How does the brain predict movement?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why do we stop noticing our own touch?",
      "a": "The brain dims sensations it expects, like your own fingers moving or clothes rubbing as you walk. This leaves your attention free for unexpected touches, which might matter more.",
      "asks": [
        "Why don't I feel my clothes all day?",
        "How does the brain ignore expected feelings?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Can a robot help you tickle yourself?",
      "a": "In experiments, people moved a lever that made a robot stroke their hand. When the scientists added a short delay, the touch no longer matched the brain's prediction, and it felt more ticklish.",
      "asks": [
        "Tickle robot experiment",
        "Does a delay make tickling work?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why do we laugh when tickled?",
      "a": "Scientists are not completely sure. Laughing at tickling may be a reflex linked to play and bonding. Young apes laugh when tickled too, and rats make high-pitched chirps.",
      "asks": [
        "Why does tickling make you laugh?",
        "Do animals laugh when tickled?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Experiments led by Sarah-Jayne Blakemore in the late 1990s found that delaying a robot's touch after a person's own movement made it feel more ticklish.",
    "The cerebellum holds more than half of all the nerve cells in the human brain."
